= Introduction
// Hook: the two shapes robot brains take today, and why neither learns from its own life.
// Third shape: organs, innate drives, surprise, sleep — biology's recipe.
// Beyond biology: inheritance of what was learned; fleet sleep; rollback; honest introspection.
// Why now: the re-org made every organ pure, so the robot's whole life is a dataset.

Contributions:
+ *An architecture.* A typed bus, pure step organs and a body interface that let one
  brain run live, from tape, in simulation and in dreams, on a K1 and a Unitree G1.
+ *Surprise and modulators.* A single prediction-error stream and three global
  modulators (arousal, fatigue, curiosity) in place of scattered thresholds.
+ *Three pre-registered experiments* on sleep, inheritance and surprise (E1–E3),
  plus a lesion study.
+ *An open release:* bus, step runtime, tapes and an organ-swap benchmark.
